{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act statutory grounds for eviction","requirement":"A landlord may end a residential tenancy only for the statutory causes, serving the Notice to Quit the cause requires (3 days to 3 years); some causes require relocation assistance, illegal-occupancy evictions require six times the monthly rent.","key_value":"Notice to Quit 3 days to 3 years by cause","coverage_conditions":"Most residential rentals: single-family homes, mobile homes and mobile home park land, apartment buildings and complexes, and rooming and boarding homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Excludes 2-3 unit owner-occupied premises with 2 or fewer rental units"},"exemptions":"Two- or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two or fewer rental units; hotel, motel or guest-house transient or seasonal guests; a unit held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate family member who permanently occupies it.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"If the landlord wants to permanently retire a building or mobile home park from residential use, provided the circumstances covered under section (G) above do not apply, the landlord may file suit for eviction. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least 18 months prior to filing the suit for eviction.","interaction":"Rent-increase evictions require the increase to comply with other laws or municipal ordinances, including rent control.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Ban on self-help evictions and lockouts","requirement":"A landlord may not padlock, disconnect utilities, block entry or remove a tenant without a court judgment; only a court officer may physically evict after a warrant for removal.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental premises in New Jersey; the ban on self-help eviction applies under any circumstances.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A tenant illegally evicted may recover possession and all damages, including court costs and reasonable attorney fees; the landlord may also be charged with disorderly conduct.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"“Self-help” eviction is entry into a dwelling unit and removal of tenants without their consent or without a judgment from a court, are not permitted in New Jersey under any circumstances.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
